.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt BUSYBOX-SHELL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm busybox-shell ,
.Nm ash ,
.Nm cttyhack ,
.Nm hush ,
.Nm "busybox sh"
.Nd BusyBox shells and controlling terminal tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ash
.Op Fl il
.Op Fl |+Cabefmnuvx
.Op Fl |+o Ar OPT
.Ar ...
.Oo
.Fl c Ar 'SCRIPT' Op Ar ARG0 ARGS
.No \&| Ar FILE ARGS
.No \&| Fl s Ar ARGS
.Oc
.Nm cttyhack
.Op Ar PROG ARGS
.Nm hush
.Op Fl enxl
.Oo
.Fl c Ar 'SCRIPT' Op Ar ARG0 ARGS
.No \&| Ar FILE ARGS
.No \&| Fl s Ar ARGS
.Oc
.Nm sh
.Op Fl il
.Op Fl |+Cabefmnuvx
.Op Fl |+o Ar OPT
.Ar ...
.Oo
.Fl c Ar 'SCRIPT' Op Ar ARG0 ARGS
.No \&| Ar FILE ARGS
.No \&| Fl s Ar ARGS
.Oc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are the shell applets of BusyBox 1.36.1 as built for Rach2.
.Bl -tag -width cttyhack
.It Nm ash , sh
Run the shell described in
.Xr sh 1 .
.It Nm cttyhack
Give
.Ar PROG
a controlling terminal if possible.
.It Nm hush
Run the hush Unix shell interpreter.
Rach2 builds it in addition to ash.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The BusyBox usage text gives this
.Pa /etc/inittab
entry for BusyBox init:
.Dl ::respawn:/bin/cttyhack /bin/sh
.Pp
Give a shell running as process ID 1 a controlling terminal:
.Dl exec cttyhack sh
.Pp
Start an interactive shell from a boot shell script:
.Dl setsid cttyhack sh
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1
.Sh HISTORY
The applet descriptions are adapted from BusyBox 1.36.1 documentation,
licensed under GPL-2.0.
